INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - EARLY MORNING

A big scarred table. Copper pots on hooks. A wood stove ticking. Through the window, mist lies low over a field of frosted stubble.

GRANDMOTHER AGNIESZKA "BABCIA" KOWALCZYK-EZE, 70s, in a faded housedress, stands at the stove stirring a pot of porridge. Her grandson FELIX DELACROIX-ADEYEMI, 11, sits at the table in pajamas, chin on his folded arms. He watches her back with the stubborn patience of a boy who has a question.

FELIX
Babcia.

BABCIA
Hm.

FELIX
Why did Mama leave?

Babcia's spoon stops. Resumes. She doesn't turn. Steam curls around her shoulders. She adds a pinch of salt, tastes, adds another.

BABCIA
Eat first.

FELIX
I'm not hungry.

BABCIA
You will be.

She ladles porridge into a chipped bowl. Brings it to the table. Sets it before him with a spoon. Smooths his hair with one flour-dusted hand. He leans into it, then catches himself and pulls away. She sits across from him with a groan, folds her hands.

BABCIA (CONT'D)
Your mother wanted a different life.

FELIX
Different from what?

BABCIA
From this.

She gestures at the kitchen, the stove, the window. Felix looks at each in turn. He picks up the spoon and stirs the porridge without eating.

FELIX
Is it that bad?

BABCIA
Not to me.

FELIX
Is it bad to you?

BABCIA
(a long pause)
No, Felix. It is not bad.

He eats a bite. Then another. She watches. The stove ticks. A rooster crows somewhere in the mist.

FELIX
Babcia?

BABCIA
Yes, my little bird?

FELIX
Will she come back?

Babcia reaches across the table and covers his hand with her own. Her knuckles are swollen, her skin like old paper. He turns his palm up and holds on.

BABCIA
Every spring the swallows go away, and every spring they return. Nobody tells them to. They only remember where the barn is.

FELIX
That's not an answer.

BABCIA
No. It isn't.

She smiles, tired and tender. He smiles back, small. Outside, the mist begins to lift, and the first gold light touches the frost.
